ntication retries to 0. The proper Cisco way to restrict access would be through AAA configuration and privilege levels.\\n\\nFor a more complete restriction of privileged access, you would typically see:\\n##\\nline vty 0 15\\n privilege level 15\\n login authentication SSH_AUTH\\n transport input ssh\\n#', 'trans_template': 'ip ssh server'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip ssh server authentication-retries 0', 'parent_command': ['username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $1$abc123$xyz4567890abcdefghijk'], 'root': 'username client001 privilege 15 secret 5 $1$abc123$xyz4567890abcdefghijk', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper command `set system services ssh root-login deny` to Cisco IOS:\\n\\n##\\nno ip ssh server\\n##\\n\\nOr alternatively (more precise equivalent):\\n##\\nip ssh server\\nip ssh server authentication-retries 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn\\'t have a direct \"root-login deny\" equivalent since Cisco IOS doesn\\'t have a root user in the same way Junos does. The most accurate translation would be to disable SSH completely (`no ip ssh server`) or make SSH logins impossible by setting authentication retries to 0. The proper Cisco way to restrict access would be through AAA configuration and privilege levels.\\n\\nFor a more complete restriction of privileged access, you would typically see:\\n##\\nline vty 0 15\\n privilege level 15\\n login authentication SSH_AUTH\\n transport input ssh\\n#', 'trans_template': 'ip ssh server authentication-retries 0'}]]": 1,
